      },
      "text": "27 December: Ainsworth Bay and the Tucker Islets"
    },
    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:fb5db666cae6efb7",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "374631a5bf70ae14805e099542cf7545d5551261293a5aa82ca064234aca4877",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-20261109-a769ac8379f2d25c8c252a41",
        "model": "GPT-6"
      },
      "text": "Early in the morning, the ship enters Admiralty Sound, an arm of the Strait of Magellan.\n\nTo the north are the snowy peaks of Karukinka Natural Park. To the south lie the fjords and bays of Alberto de Agostini National Park.\n\nYou'll go ashore at Ainsworth Bay in small Zodiac boats.\n\nBirds and elephant seals are found in the area.\n\nThere are two walking routes to choose from.\n\nThe first takes you through subpolar forest, past streams, waterfalls and moss-covered rocks.\n\nThe second is more challenging and follows the ridge of a glacial moraine.\n\nThe route offers views of Marinelli Glacier and the Darwin Range.\n\nBack on board, the ship sails to the Tucker Islets.\n\nAfter lunch, another Zodiac landing is scheduled.\n\nMagellanic penguins use the islands to nest and raise their chicks.\n\nIn September and April, penguins are usually absent. In that case, the visit to the islets is replaced with a walk to a glacier in neighbouring Brooks Bay."
    },
